The W213 adopts Mercedes’ modular MRA platform, as used by the contemporary C-Class and S-Class models. The W213 E-Class grew 43&nbsp;mm in length to {{convert|4923|mm|in|0|abbr=on|}}. Wheelbase was also extended 65&nbsp;mm to {{convert|2939|mm|in|0|abbr=on|}}, resulting in shorter overhangs. It is 2&nbsp;mm narrower and 6&nbsp;mm lower than its predecessor.<ref name="E-Class revealed">{{Cite news |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-detroit-motor-show/new-mercedes-e-class-revealed-plus-video |title=New Mercedes-Benz E-Class - exclusive pictures and video |newspaper=Autocar |access-date=11 January 2016 |archive-date=12 January 2016 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20160112082005/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-detroit-motor-show/new-mercedes-e-class-revealed-plus-video |url-status=live }}</ref> The increased use of high-strength steel and aluminium is claimed to make the car up to 100&nbsp;kg lighter than the W212 E-Class.<ref name="2016eclassautocar">{{cite web|url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/2016-mercedes-benz-e-class-spotted-engines-and-launch-date|title=2016 Mercedes-Benz E-Class - engines and launch date|author=Greg Kable|work=Autocar|access-date=8 July 2015|archive-date=26 July 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20150726151952/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/2016-mercedes-benz-e-class-spotted-engines-and-launch-date|url-status=live}}</ref>

The W213 E-Class is based on the [[Mercedes-Benz F800|F800 Style]] design language, previously used on the larger W222 S-Class (2013) and the smaller W205 C-Class (2014).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.carscoops.com/2016/01/we-visually-compare-mercedes-c-e-and-s.html|title=We Visually-Compare Mercedes' C-, E- And S-Class Sedans|website=Carscoops|access-date=23 May 2016}}</ref> However, the facelifted W212 (2013) also incorporates several design cues from the F800 Style, thus the two vehicles share several design traits.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://indianautosblog.com/2016/01/mercedes-e-class-w213-vs-w212-old-vs-new-210378|title = 2016 Mercedes e Class (W213) vs Mercedes e Class (W212)|website=Indianautosblog.com|date = 7 January 2016}}</ref>

The W213 E-Class' front bumper is reminiscent of the facelifted W212's optional sport kit (dubbed the AMG Sports Package), as it is governed by two air intakes united by a mesh grille. Compared to the standard front bumper of the W212, which is similar to the one used on the W205 C-Class, the W213 E-Class' proposal is more aggressive, composed and simple.<ref name="W213vsW212">{{cite web |url=http://www.carscoops.com/2016/01/evolution-or-revolution-new-vs-old.html |title=Evolution Or Revolution? New Vs Old Mercedes-Benz E-Class |website=Carscoops.com |date=18 January 2016 |access-date=12 August 2022}}</ref> The classic iconic grille, has simpler, flowing lines, with a more imposing presence on W213, while the sport variant (with the big logo in the middle) isn't so different from the W212. The S-Class inspired, sweptback (first time in Mercedes executive-class history) and rather compact headlights are the styling feature of the W213 model.<ref name="W213vsW212"/>

On both cars the doors sit pretty high, squeezing the windows, but the W212 masks this "trait" with a subtle slope towards the headlights. In fact, on the profile of the facelifted W212, it still retains the edgy lines that came out in 2009.<ref name="W213vsW212"/> At the back, the W213 adopted a fresh design for the lights similar to the S-Class. However, the tailpipes finishers are similar to the ones found on the W212, with the rear bumper keeping its overall shape.<ref name="W213vsW212"/>

=== Interior ===
[[File:Mercedes-Benz E-Klasse (2016) W213.jpg|thumb|right|The interior with two 12.3-inch full HD screens housed within a single unit.]]
The interior of the 2017 E-Class was unveiled by Mercedes-Benz in December 2015, which was previewed on the Concept IAA (Intelligent Aerodynamic Automobile) that Mercedes showed in September 2015 at the [[Frankfurt Auto show]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.autonews.com/article/20151103/OEM04/151109978/2017-mercedes-benz-e-class-borrows-styling-cues-from-s-class-flagship|title=2017 Mercedes-Benz E-Class borrows styling cues from S-Class flagship|author=Jack Walsworth|work=Automotive News|date=2 November 2015|access-date=3 November 2015|archive-date=3 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201003123323/https://www.autonews.com/article/20151103/OEM04/151109978/2017-mercedes-benz-e-class-borrows-styling-cues-from-s-class-flagship|url-status=live}}</ref> The interior mounts two 12.3-inch full HD screens housed within a single unit.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.carsguide.com.au/car-news/2016-mercedes-e-class-digital-dash-revealed-37337|title=2016 Mercedes E-Class digital dash revealed|author=John Carey|work=CarsGuide|access-date=15 December 2015|archive-date=17 December 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20151217224741/http://www.carsguide.com.au/car-news/2016-mercedes-e-class-digital-dash-revealed-37337|url-status=live}}</ref> Most E-Class models receive the widescreen set-up as standard. However, base Avantgarde versions features traditional analogue dials with a seven-inch multi-information display, along with an 8.4-inch central infotainment screen.<ref name="interiorcaradvice">{{cite web|url=http://www.caradvice.com.au/401853/2016-mercedes-benz-e-class-interior-revealed/|title=2016 Mercedes-Benz E-Class interior revealed|work=CarAdvice.com.au|access-date=15 December 2015|archive-date=17 December 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20151217223631/http://www.caradvice.com.au/401853/2016-mercedes-benz-e-class-interior-revealed/|url-status=live}}</ref> Mercedes-Benz claimed a first for the automotive industry, adding a pair of touch-sensitive control buttons on the steering wheel, which respond to finger swipes to control the car's infotainment system.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://jalopnik.com/the-2017-mercedes-benz-e-class-has-all-the-display-scre-1747795067|title=The 2017 Mercedes-Benz E-Class Has All The Display Screens|author=Alanis King|work=Jalopnik|date=13 December 2015 |access-date=15 December 2015|archive-date=17 December 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20151217225534/http://jalopnik.com/the-2017-mercedes-benz-e-class-has-all-the-display-scre-1747795067|url-status=live}}</ref> The more traditional [[Mercedes-Benz COMAND]] controller is also retained on the centre console.<ref name="interiorcaradvice"/>

== Equipment ==
=== Autonomous driving ===
A number of sensors, cameras and radars has allowed semi-automated driving. There may be fewer sensors than before (12 and four respectively over the W212 model), but they all feature increased functionality which reduces cost and complexity.<ref name="techspecs">{{cite web|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mercedes/e-class/89072/mercedes-e-class-2016-tech-secrets-pics-and-specs|title=Mercedes E-Class 2016: tech secrets, pics and specs|work=Auto Express}}</ref>

The next level of Drive Pilot (code-named Intelligent Drive) featured on the 2017 E-Class enables the car to negotiate bends on the motorway, while maintaining a safe distance from slower moving vehicles in front at up to speeds of {{Convert|130|mph|km/h|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.alphr.com/cars/1002399/new-mercedes-benz-e-class-this-saloon-can-drive-itself-at-130mph-and-prevent-crashes |title=New Mercedes-Benz E-Class: This saloon can drive itself at 130mph and PREVENT crashes |date=11 January 2016 |access-date=11 January 2016 |archive-date=12 January 2016 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20160112082808/http://www.alphr.com/cars/1002399/new-mercedes-benz-e-class-this-saloon-can-drive-itself-at-130mph-and-prevent-crashes |url-status=live }}</ref> The system is not a hands-free operation and an audible alert will prompt the driver into regaining control if the car detects their attention has wandered.<ref name="techspecs"/>

The Steering Pilot function uses visible road markings to navigate bends at speeds of up to {{Convert|81|mph|km/h|abbr=on}}. The car is able to scan the surrounding area, as well as the car in front, if road markings are unclear to maintain lane discipline.<ref name="techspecs"/> The system is also able to automatically increase or decrease the E-Class's speed by reading speed limit signs if the driver fails to take any action.<ref name="techspecs"/>

=== Safety innovations ===
Active Brake Assist and Evasive Steering Assist work in tandem to warn of and prevent potential collisions in the 2017 E-Class. The first system now features increased functionality, able to provide visible warning of potential danger as well as being able to automatically apply the brakes in an emergency. The system is now also able to detect and analyze moving traffic at junctions ahead. Evasive Steering Assist can detect when a driver is making an evasive action and apply additional steering force to ensure the pedestrian or vehicle is avoided. Car-to-X Communication is another safety system which enables vehicles on the same road to warn each other of upcoming potential hazards or accidents.<ref name="techspecs"/>

If an unavoidable side collision is detected, PRE-SAFE Impulse Side rapidly inflates the side bolster to move the occupant away from the point of impact, while at the same time PRE-SAFE Sound pumps noise waves through the speakers to reduce hearing damage following a collision.<ref name="techspecs"/>

=== Adaptive LED matrix lighting ===
[[File:Mercedes-Benz E 250 Stationwagon AVANTGARDE Sports （S213, leather seat）front (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|Adaptive LED matrix lighting]]
Introduced on the [[Mercedes-Benz CLS-Class (W218)|CLS]] for 2014, Mercedes’ [[LED headlamp|Multibeam LED]] headlamps are enhanced for the 2017 E-class. The individual LED count increases from 24 to 84, each individually controllable—switching between high- and low-beams, and the curve-following adaptive-lighting functions are now achieved entirely via electronics. The light pattern is also altered in city driving or via information from the navigation system.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blog.caranddriver.com/so-much-tech-detailing-the-2017-mercedes-benz-e-classs-gadgetry/|title=2017 Mercedes-Benz E-class: Tons of New Technology – News – Car and Driver - Car and Driver Blog|work=caranddriver.com|date=7 July 2015|access-date=8 July 2015|archive-date=26 July 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20150726151949/http://blog.caranddriver.com/so-much-tech-detailing-the-2017-mercedes-benz-e-classs-gadgetry/|url-status=live}}</ref>

As with the mid-cycle refresh of [[Mercedes-Benz E-Class (W212)#W212 facelift (2013–2016)|W212]], the 2017 E-Class features two larger units instead of the four-eyes headlights, though LED light bars within the headlamps still gives the night-time effect of four individual units.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.autoguide.com/auto-news/2012/12/2014-mercedes-e-class-says-bu-bye-four-eyes.html|title=2014 Mercedes E-Class Says Buh-Bye Four-Eyes|author=Colum Wood|date=13 December 2012|work=AutoGuide.com News|access-date=9 September 2015|archive-date=7 December 2015|archive-url=https://archive.today/20151207141622/http://www.autoguide.com/auto-news/2012/12/2014-mercedes-e-class-says-bu-bye-four-eyes.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Drift mode ===
Introduced in the [[Mercedes-AMG]] E 63 S [[4Matic|4MATIC+]], drift mode allows the car to completely cut the front axle from its all-wheel-drive 4MATIC system and transfer all of the power to the rear axle of the car. This allows the driver to engage in easier drifts.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.carmagazine.co.uk/car-news/tech/does-it-work-mercedes-amg-e63-ss-drift-mode-tested/|title=Does it work? Mercedes-AMG E63 S's drift mode tested|work=CAR Magazine|access-date=15 May 2018|language=en}}</ref>

== Variants ==
{{More citations needed section|date=September 2018}}

=== Coupé and Cabriolet ===
The Coupé (C238) was first unveiled by Mercedes at the 2017 [[North American International Auto Show]], while the Cabriolet (A238) was unveiled at the 2017 [[Geneva International Motor Show]], both to be released for the 2018 model year.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Mercedes unveils 2018 E-Class coupe ahead of Detroit auto show |url=https://driving.ca/mercedes-benz/e-class/auto-shows/detroit-auto-show/mercedes-benz-unveils-2018-e-class-coupe-ahead-of-detroit-auto-show |access-date=15 May 2023 |website=driving |language=en-CA}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Mercedes-Benz E-Class Cabriolet unveiled |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-geneva-motor-show/mercedes-benz-e-class-cabriolet-unveiled |access-date=15 May 2023 |website=Autocar |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=1 March 2017 |title=2018 Mercedes-Benz E-class Cabriolet Revealed, Gains Elegance, Space, and Style |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a15097365/2018-mercedes-benz-e-class-cabriolet-photos-and-info-news/ |access-date=15 May 2023 |website=Car and Driver |language=en-US}}</ref> Both models replaced the previous [[Mercedes-Benz E-Class#Coupe and convertible (C207/A207)|C207/A207 E-Class]] models. Like the E-Class coupé it replaced, the C238 coupé features a [[Hardtop#Pillarless hardtop|pillarless]] design with no [[B pillar]] between the front and rear side windows, though there is a small pillar between the main rear windows and the fixed [[Quarter glass|quarter glass]] windows behind them.

The Coupé and Cabriolet were both initially offered in 2018 only as an E 400 model with the [[Mercedes-Benz M276 engine|M276]] 3.0L Biturbo V6 engine producing {{cvt|333|PS|hp kW|0}} and {{Convert|480|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque.<ref name=":0" />

Select markets also offered the E 200 model with an M274 engine 2.0L turbo I4 engine producing {{cvt|184|PS|hp kW|0}}, and the E 300 model with an M274 2.0L turbo I4 engine producing {{Convert|245|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}}.

For 2019 onwards, the E 400 model was replaced by the E 450 model, featuring the same M276 3.0-litre Biturbo V6 engine, but now producing {{cvt|362|PS|hp kW|0}} and {{Convert|500|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}}.

