In a departure from previous generations, the Estate is offered in two distinct variants: a traditional on-road model and an All-Terrain version to directly rival the [[Audi A6 Allroad]] and [[Volvo V90 Cross Country]].<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-paris-motor-show/mercedes-e-class-all-terrain-estate-revealed| title = Mercedes E-Class All-Terrain estate revealed {{!}} Autocar}}</ref>

Unveiled at the 2016 [[Paris Motor Show]], the Estate All-Terrain features a 29&nbsp;mm higher ride height due to the Air Body Control air suspension (15&nbsp;mm) and 20 in wheels fitted as standard (14&nbsp;mm). There is also external plastic cladding to protect the body from scratches and scrapes, as well as the 4Matic permanent four-wheel-drive system with 31/69 front-rear torque split.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/mercedes-benz/e-class/first-drives/mercedes-benz-e-class-all-terrain-2017-uk-review|title=Mercedes-Benz E-Class All-Terrain 2017 UK review
|website=Autocar|access-date=14 November 2017}}</ref>

=== E 300 (2016–2023) ===
An E 300, with a 2.0-litre turbo petrol engine, joined the range. This engine was previously available in the facelift W212 E-class in some markets. This engine produces {{Convert|184|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} in the E 200, {{Convert|211|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} in the E 250 and {{Convert|245|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} in the E 300. Like the W212 facelift, the W213 E 300 produces {{Convert|245|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|370|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} and accelerates from {{convert|0|-|100|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} in 6.2 seconds.

=== E 350 (2019–2023) ===
The E 350, with an updated 2.0-litre turbo petrol engine, joined the E-Class range for the 2020 model year. This engine is also used in the facelift W205 C-Class and X253/C253 GLC-Class, as well as the W167 GLE-Class. In the E 350, it produces {{Convert|299|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} at 5,800 - 6,100 rpm and {{Convert|400|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} at 3,000 - 4,000 rpm. Selected markets such as Malaysia and Australia have the EQ Boost feature available on this engine, which produces an additional {{Convert|14|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}}. It has a {{Convert|0|-|100|kph|mph|abbr=on}} acceleration time of 5.9 seconds.<ref>[https://www.mercedes-benz.co.id/passengercars/being-an-owner/brochure-price/md-saloon/_jcr_content/par/interactions.attachments.1.E-Class_W%20123_E200_E300_E350_Brochure_Tablet.pdf Brochure] mercedes-benz.co.id</ref>

=== E 400 (2016–2019) ===
An E 400 with a 3.0-litre twin-turbo M276 V6 petrol engine. It is carried over from the W212 facelift E class and it produces {{Convert|333|PS|hp kW|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|480|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque. It accelerates from 0–{{convert|100|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 4.9 seconds, a full second faster than the E 350.

The E400 was released with different packages, Standard, Avantgarde, Exclusive, AMG-Line & Night / Black Sport.

The Avantgarde, AMG-Line & Night featured an updated grille with the Mercedes Star.

The Exclusive incorporated a woodgrain dash inside.

Depending on the market, not all models were available. The Japanese market only sold the Exclusive in the E400.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 May 2017 |title=モデル - The E-Class - メルセデス・ベンツ Eクラス セダン |url=http://www.mercedes-benz.co.jp/content/japan/mpc/mpc_japan_website/ja/home_mpc/passengercars/home/new_cars/models/e-class/w213/facts/models.html |access-date=24 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170514192533/http://www.mercedes-benz.co.jp/content/japan/mpc/mpc_japan_website/ja/home_mpc/passengercars/home/new_cars/models/e-class/w213/facts/models.html |archive-date=14 May 2017 }}</ref>

=== E 400 Wagon (2016–2019) ===
[[File:Mercedes-Benz E 400 4MATIC, IAA 2017, Frankfurt (1Y7A3001).jpg|thumb|right|E 400 Wagon]]
The E 400 wagon features the same 3.0-litre M276 biturbo [[V6 engine|V6]] petrol engine as the sedan version. This car shares many similarities with the sedan but shows differences in its styling and engine. Unlike the E 400 sedan's single turbo engine, the E 400 wagon is equipped with a twin turbo V6 producing {{Convert|333|PS|kW hp|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|354|lbft|Nm|abbr=on}} of [[torque]]. The main difference seen in this particular model is the fact that it has an increased cargo area. The E 400 wagon has a maximum space of 64.3 cubic feet of load space compared to with 57.4 in the sedan version.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.motortrend.com/cars/mercedes-benz/e-class/2017/2017-mercedes-benz-e400-4matic-wagon-first-drive-review/|title=2017 Mercedes-Benz E 400 Wagon Review: The World's Best Wagon|date=12 September 2016|work=Motor Trend|access-date=7 May 2018|language=en-US}}</ref> The E 400 wagon can also be equipped through options with third row seating allowing more passengers to ride in the car. It can accelerate from {{convert|0|-|60|mph|km/h|abbr=on}} in 5.1 seconds and do the quarter mile in 13.7 seconds at {{Convert|102|mph|km/h|abbr=on}} due to its [[4Matic]] [[All-wheel drive|all wheel drive]] system.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.motortrend.com/cars/mercedes-benz/e-class/2017/2017-mercedes-benz-e400-4matic-wagon-first-test-focused-family/|title=2017 Mercedes-Benz E400 4Matic Wagon First Test: Focused on the Family - Motor Trend|date=6 June 2017|work=Motor Trend|access-date=7 May 2018|language=en-US}}</ref>

=== E 450 (2019–2023) ===
For the 2019 model year, the E 400 was renamed to E 450. It features the same [[Mercedes-Benz M276 engine|M276]] 3.0L Biturbo V6 engine with slightly higher outputs, producing {{Convert|362|PS|kW hp|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|500|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}}. For the 2020 facelift, the [[Mercedes-Benz M276 engine|M276]] Biturbo V6 was dropped for the new [[Mercedes-Benz M256 engine|M256]] 3.0L Inline 6 turbo with EQ Boost. Power outputs are unchanged at {{Convert|362|PS|kW hp|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|500|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}}.

=== E 350e Plug-in Hybrid (2016–2023) ===
[[File:2016-03-01 Geneva Motor Show 1000.JPG|thumb|right|E 350e Plug-in Hybrid]]
The E 350e is a [[plug-in hybrid]], equipped with a {{Convert|208|hp|PS kW|abbr=on}} turbocharged inline-four coupled with an {{Convert|87|hp|kW PS|abbr=on}} electric motor. The E 350e's transmission is a nine-speed automatic gearbox. Mercedes claims that the car has a {{Convert|21|mi|km|abbr=on}} electric only range. The hybrid plug to charge the car is connected on the rear bumper through a small door similar to how the gas door is set up. The E 350e can accelerate from {{convert|0|-|60|mph|km/h|abbr=on}} in 6.2 seconds. Mercedes claims the E 350e weighs about {{Convert|4250|lb|kg|abbr=on}}, compared to the E 300's weight of {{Convert|3650|lb|kg|abbr=on}}. The way to distinguish an E 350e from a non-hybrid E 300 is the badging.{{Citation needed|date=September 2018}} An E 350e badge is placed on the rear trunk as well as a blue "Plug-in Hybrid" on the side of the car. Mercedes offers an option to paint the brake calipers the same blue as the "Plug-in Hybrid" as well.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/2018-mercedes-benz-e350e-plug-in-hybrid-first-drive-review|title=2018 Mercedes-Benz E350e Plug-In Hybrid First Drive {{!}} Review {{!}} Car and Driver|work=Car and Driver|access-date=8 May 2018|language=en}}</ref>

===<span class="anchor" id="V213"></span> Long wheelbase (V213; China and India)===
[[File:Mercedes-Benz E-Class V213 01 China 2017-03-19.jpg|thumb|The Chinese market E-Class LWB]]

The '''V213''' is the latest generation long wheelbase E-Class saloon. This Long-Wheelbase version so far is only available in the Chinese and Indian markets. The E-Class V213 was launched at the 2016 [[Beijing Auto Show]] and went on sale in China in August the same year. The V213 E-Class is often referred to as the ‘smaller S-Class’, mainly due to the many similarities they have including the shape of the [[Pillar (car)|c-pillar]] and so on.

The car is 5,065&nbsp;mm long, which makes it the longest E-Class ever made. The V213 E-Class is sold in three basic models, E200L, E300L and E320L, all of which can be selected between Exclusive line and AMG Line designs. The new 4-cylinder turbocharged M274 or M276 petrol engines are available, which are quick enough to accelerate the E200L from 0-{{convert|62|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 8.6 seconds, the E300L in 6.9 seconds and the E320L 4MATIC in a claimed 5.7 seconds. The new 9G-Tronic transmission, which focuses more on efficiency and smoother gear changes, is standard across the whole range.

On 29 September 2020, Mercedes also unveiled the facelifted version of V213 LWB E-Class at the [[Auto China]] 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/446312/2021-mercedes-e-class-long-wheelbase/|title = 2021 Mercedes E-Class Long Wheelbase Debuts with Generous Legroom}}</ref> Like before, this is only sold in China and India.

[[File:Mercedes-Benz E-Class V213 002 China 2019-03-14.jpg|thumb|left|Rear view]]

Since its launch, there have been some disappointment as well as admiration in China. The longer wheelbase and luxury equipment have made the new E-Class popularly recognized as the less expensive alternative to the S-Class. Another reason being that the design has taken inspiration from the Maybach S-Class. While some buyers are dissatisfied with the fact that some of the equipment like the multibeam headlights and several driving assistances, which are brand new features on this model, do not come as standard and are listed as "optional extras" (to pay for), others comment that the spacious cabin, new interior (all V213 E-Class have 12.3 inch displays) and luxurious features such as onboard [[Wi-Fi]] and [[Wireless Charging]], and automatic emergency brakes as standard, which are items not available on the worldwide W213 E-Class contribute to the Long-Wheelbase's market success. The price difference between W213 and V213 E-Class in China is not significantly large, around only 10,000 RMB ($1,560), which makes the V213 E-Class even more attractive to buy.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mercedes-benz.com.cn/content/china/mpc/mpc_china_website/zhng/home_mpc/passengercars.html|title=Mercedes-Benz China}}</ref>

