==Mercedes-AMG models ==
=== AMG E 43 (2016–2018) ===
{{multiple image
 | total_width       = 350
 | align             = right
 | caption_align     = center
 | image1            = 2017 Mercedes-AMG E43 Premium+ 4MATIC 3.0 Front.jpg
 | caption1          = 
 | image2            = 2017 Mercedes-AMG E43 Premium+ 4MATIC 3.0 Rear.jpg
 | caption2          = 
 | footer_background = 
 | footer_align      = center
 | footer            = Mercedes-Benz AMG E 43 Premium+
}}

The all-wheel-drive only Mercedes-AMG E 43 joined the range in September 2016, powered by a {{convert|401|PS|kW bhp||abbr=on}} 3.0-litre Biturbo V6, producing {{convert|521|Nm|lbft||abbr=on}} of torque, mated to a nine-speed automatic transmission. Acceleration from 0 to {{convert|100|km/h|mph||abbr=on}} takes 4.6 seconds. Top speed is electronically limited to {{convert|250|km/h|mph||abbr=on}}.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-new-york-motor-show/mercedes-amg-e43-4matic-revealed-new-york-motor-show| title = Mercedes-AMG E43 4Matic revealed at New York motor show {{!}} Autocar}}</ref> The AMG E 43 was the first of the new AMG 43 series with a higher output of 401 PS vs 367 PS of the existing AMG 43 series. The E 43 was dropped for the 2018 model year, replaced by the E 53. Since the discontinuation of the E 43 in 2018, an E 400 (later renamed to E 450) was launched to fill the gap between the 4-cylinder E300 and the 6 cylinder, high-performance AMG E 53.
{{clear right}}

=== AMG E 53 4MATIC+ (2018–2023) ===
{{multiple image
 | total_width       = 350
 | align             = right
 | caption_align     = center
 | image1            = Mercedes-AMG E 53 (W213) IMG 3225.jpg
 | caption1          = E 53 4MATIC+
 | image2            = Mercedes-AMG E 53 (W213) FL IMG 3965.jpg
 | caption2          = E 53 4MATIC+ Facelift
 | footer_background = 
 | footer_align      = center
 | footer            = 
}}
A mid-level AMG version, E 53 4MATIC+, is fitted with a 3-litre 6-cylinder inline [[Mercedes-Benz M256 engine|M256 E30 DEH LA G]] engine, producing {{cvt|320|kW|PS hp|0}} and {{cvt|520|Nm|lbft|0}}. Due to the higher volume of mid-level AMG version, this type of engine is tuned by AMG and built on the assembly lines by people and robots at the manufacturing plants rather than "one man, one engine" approach.

The Mercedes-AMG Coupé and Cabriolet versions were introduced once again in 2019 after skipping [[Mercedes-Benz E-Class (C207)|the previous generation (C207)]]. AMG E 53 4MATIC+ remains the sole AMG version for both coupé and cabriolet.

=== AMG E 53 4MATIC+ (2026) ===
In July 2026, Mercedes-Benz India launched the AMG E 53 HYBRID 4MATIC+ in Performance and Racing editions, priced from ₹1.45 crore (ex-showroom). Its 3.0-litre inline-six plug-in hybrid produces 585 hp (up to 612 hp with RACE START) and accelerates from 0–100 km/h in 3.8 to 4.0 seconds, offering over 100 km of electric-only range.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mynextcar.in/prices/Mercedes-Benz/AMG_E_53|title=Mercedes-Benz AMG E 53: Variants & Price|website=Mynextcar.in|access-date=26 August 2026}}</ref>

===AMG E 63 4MATIC+ and AMG E 63 S 4MATIC+ (2016–2023)===
{{multiple image
 | total_width       = 350
 | align             = right
 | caption_align     = center
 | image1            = Mercedes, Paris Motor Show 2018, Paris (1Y7A1870).jpg
 | caption1          = E 63 4MATIC+
 | image2            = 2019 Mercedes-Benz E-Class E 63 AMG S 4MATIC Wagon, front left.jpg
 | caption2          = E 63 S 4MATIC+ Wagon
 | footer_background = 
 | footer_align      = center
 | footer            = 
}}

Presented in November 2016, the new Mercedes-AMG E 63 4MATIC+ and AMG E 63 S 4MATIC+ received AMG [[Mercedes-Benz M177 engine|M177]] twin-turbo 4.0-litre V8 in two states of tune and with fully variable all-wheel-drive. The standard AMG E 63 4MATIC+ has {{convert|571|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} and accelerates from 0 to {{convert|100|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 3.5 seconds. The AMG E 63 S 4MATIC+ has same engine but with higher output rate at {{convert|612|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} and accelerates from 0 to {{convert|100|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 3.4 seconds. Both versions are electronically limited to {{convert|250|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}}, however this can be increased to {{Convert|300|km/h|0|abbr=on}} with the AMG Driver's Package.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-la-motor-show/2017-mercedes-amg-e-63-arrives-la-quickest-amg-yet| title = 2017 Mercedes-AMG E 63 pricing and specs announced {{!}} Autocar}}</ref> While most other high-performance AMG variants such as the E53 have had electrification added in recent years, the E63 stands out as one which didn't receive such a performance-enhancing 48V mild-hybrid system.<ref name=":1">{{cite web|last=Ewing|first=Steven|title=Mercedes-AMG explains why the 2021 E63 uses the same powertrain as before|url=https://www.cnet.com/roadshow/news/2021-mercedes-benz-amg-e63-sedan-wagon-engine-updates/|access-date=6 July 2021|website=Roadshow|language=en}}</ref> Director of Vehicle Development at Mercedes-AMG, Drummond Jacoy, gave two main reasons, physical space and market positioning. "We want a Race Start [launch control feature], we want quick shifting," Jacoy said. "To do that we've used our electromechanical clutch and not a torque converter," the latter of which works with the integrated starter-generator (ISG) setup found in the EQ-Boost mild-hybrid tech. "To be completely honest with you, we have package restraints, so we couldn't fit the ISG in there."<ref name=":1" /> Secondly, "We have a car lineup," Jacoy noted, "and above the E63 we have the GT63 S, so we don't want to have an overlap there. We kept more power exclusive to the GT63 S."<ref name=":1" />

The E 63 4MATIC+ models are both also available with the wagon body. The AMG E 63 wagon's styling cues and features are much different from the base model to distinguish that it is a performance variant of the car.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.leftlanenews.com/new-car-buying/mercedes-benz/e63-amg-wagon/|title=2016 Mercedes-Benz E63 AMG 4MATIC Wagon Specs, Photos, Prices|website=LeftLaneNews|language=en-US|access-date=7 May 2018}}</ref> The front bumper is wider, a front splitter is added, and large opening flanks are seen on the AMG-branded grille. The car also has various AMG branding throughout it to show its significance and the badging on the rear trunk lid depicts that it is in fact an AMG E 63 (S) wagon. The AMG E 63 wagon is equipped with 64 cubic feet of cargo volume compared to the AMG E 63 sedan's 57.4 cubic feet. The vehicle is also used as the official F1 medical car.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/2018-mercedes-amg-e63-s-wagon-photos-and-info-news|title=2018 Mercedes-AMG E63 S Wagon Photos and Info |work=Car and Driver|access-date=7 May 2018|language=en}}</ref> As with the sedan, the wagon also does not receive the mild-hybrid technology found in other cars in the AMG lineup.

== W213 facelift ==
[[File:Mercedes-Benz S213 FL IMG 3473.jpg|thumb|Wagon (facelift)]]
[[File:2020-09-22 MB-E200 W213-MOPF.jpg|thumb|Sedan (facelift)]]
[[File:Mercedes-Benz E350eL 2022-02-06-01.jpg|thumb|Chinese LWB model Facelift (E350eL)]]
[[File:2023_Mercedes-Benz_E-Class_interior.jpg|thumb|right|Interior (facelift)]]
For the 2021 model year, the E-Class has received updated front and rear fascias along with a new mild-hybrid six-cylinder inline engine for the E450 model; the All-Terrain model will now be available in the United States, replacing the standard estate version. It was supposed to be released at the 2020 [[Geneva Motor Show]], which was cancelled due to [[COVID-19]]. Inside, COMAND is replaced by MBUX with a 12.3-inch touchscreen and a new steering wheel is used. Driver-assistance technology has also been updated featuring an advanced capacitive hands-off detection system.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a31191874/2021-mercedes-benz-e-class-photos-info/|title=2021 Mercedes E-Class Expands Inline-Six Availability, Adds Hybrid|work=[[Car and Driver]]|last=Stafford|first=Eric|date=3 March 2020|access-date=3 March 2020}}</ref>

Mercedes-AMG announced on 27 May 2020 that the updated Mercedes-AMG versions of coupé and cabriolet are now available.<ref name=":2">{{cite web|url=https://www.mercedes-benz.com/en/vehicles/passenger-cars/e-class/mercedes-amg-e-53-4matic-coupe-and-cabriolet/?csref=sm_ytb_g63_w463_pc.html/|title=The new Mercedes-AMG E 53 4MATIC+ Coupé and Cabriolet |work=Mercedes-Benz |date=27 May 2020}}</ref> The E 53 4MATIC+ version remains the sole choice for both coupé and cabriolet.

In mid-2022, Mercedes-AMG revealed the Final Edition<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://paultan.org/2022/06/01/mercedes-amg-e63-s-final-edition-the-end-of-the-v8-powered-e-class-in-sedan-and-estate-form-999-units/|title=Mercedes-AMG E63 S Final Edition - the end of the V8-powered E-Class in sedan and estate form; 999 units |website=Paultan.org|date=1 June 2022|access-date=21 January 2024}}</ref> version of the E 63 S 4Matic+. Signifying the V8’s swansong in production, a total of 999 units were to be built.

